// F62: Colour adds 0.002 of explained variance to photo memorability once the subject is known
// The BuildTeX source of this whitepaper. Built with: buildc run, then python -m buildtex.
mod doc;
use doc::*;

fn main() ~ Console + FileSystem {
    let mut d = doc_new("Colour adds 0.002 of explained variance to photo memorability once the subject is known");
    theme(&mut d, "paper");
    meta(&mut d, "pdf_author", "Zain Dana Harper");
    meta(&mut d, "subject", "A preregistered test of how much three colour properties add to held-out prediction of image memorability beyond subject subcategory in the MemCat set of 9,988 photographs");
    meta(&mut d, "footer", "F62, colour and photo memorability within subject, version 1.0");
    blk(&mut d, "title", "Colour adds 0.002 of explained variance to photo memorability once the subject is known");
    blk(&mut d, "subtitle", "A preregistered test on the MemCat image set: lightness, chroma and colour temperature against recognition memory, within 134 subject subcategories, with content-matched shuffles");
    blk(&mut d, "author", "Zain Dana Harper");
    blk(&mut d, "meta", "Version 1.0, 14 November 2026. Finding F62 in the findings ledger. Tier B. Memorability arm only.");
    blk(&mut d, "abstracthead", "Abstract");
    blk(&mut d, "abstract", "Bright, saturated and warm pictures are often said to stick in memory. We tested how much of that survives once the subject of the photograph is held fixed. The data are the 9,988 colour photographs of the public MemCat set, each with a memorability score from a repeat-detection game and a label for one of 134 subject subcategories. Subcategory alone predicts held-out memorability with an R2 of 0.558. Adding lightness, chroma and colour temperature lifts held-out R2 by 0.0022 (95% interval 0.0009 to 0.0034), from 0.558 to 0.560; the content-matched shuffle reaches 0.0001 at its 95th percentile. Within a subcategory each property correlates weakly with memorability: lightness +0.027, chroma +0.041 and log colour temperature -0.056, each above a shuffle bar near 0.019. Colour alone, with no subject label, predicts memorability at R2 0.096, about 44 times the gain it adds once subcategory is known. The effect is real under our controls and small. A subcategory is a coarse label, so the remaining link may still stand for scene content such as snow or sky. This page reports one of the study's four tests; the other three are left out for the reasons given in the design section.");
    labelled(&mut d, "section", "sec:reader", "What this means for a reader");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "In this image set, most of what looks like a link between colour and memorability is a link between subject and memorability: food and animals are remembered better than landscapes, and they also differ in colour. Once the subject is fixed, colour explains well under one percent of the remaining differences. A claim that a warmer or more saturated image is more memorable should be read with the subject controlled, and with the size of the effect beside its p value.");
    labelled(&mut d, "section", "sec:intro", "Introduction");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "Memorability is the chance that a viewer who has seen a photograph once recognises it when it comes round again. Isola and colleagues showed that this chance is stable across viewers and so belongs partly to the image {cite:isola2014}. Papers that cite that work report its colour result as weak: mean hue, saturation and similar simple features predict memorability poorly. We read that through citing papers and a search summary; the paper's own tables were not read.");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "Goetschalckx and Wagemans built MemCat to study memorability inside subject categories: 10,000 photographs in five broad categories, each split into subcategories, with a memorability score for every image {cite:memcat2019}. That structure allows a question the earlier sets answer less directly. Hold the subject fixed at the subcategory level, and ask what colour still adds.");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "The decision this informed was whether a tool of ours that tunes images should carry a colour control aimed at memorability. Our searches returned the MemCat paper and the weak colour result of Isola and colleagues, and no measurement of the within-subcategory colour gain on MemCat. That is what two searches returned; it does not show that none exists.");
    labelled(&mut d, "section", "sec:prereg", "Preregistration and design");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "The preregistration was written and hash-locked on 29 October 2026, before any statistic of the study was computed. Before the lock we had seen the layout of the data files and the mean and standard deviation of MemCat memorability (0.693 and 0.137). No colour property had been correlated with anything.");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "The study held four tests. This paper reports the third, colour and memorability on MemCat, and leaves out the other three:");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "The first test compared the colour of one person's saved images with seasonal day length. It rests on a private image collection, and its planted check fell just short of its own bar, so it is not published.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "The second test, colour and mood, was recorded as untestable before the run: no mood record overlapping the images exists.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "The fourth test reused an image index and word ratings from an earlier study of ours that are not a public dataset.");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "The memorability test carried five verdicts, each a rule fixed before the run and read off by code: a sanity verdict, a gain verdict and one verdict per colour property. The rules are in Appendix A. The multiple-test correction was fixed over the eight primary p values of the whole study, four of which belong to this test.");
    labelled(&mut d, "section", "sec:data", "Data and provenance");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "MemCat {cite:memcat2019} holds 10,000 photographs in five categories (animal, food, landscape, sports, vehicle) and 134 subcategories. Its memorability score is the hit rate in a repeat-detection game corrected for false alarms, from about 99 viewers per image. The files come from the Open Science Framework node kvghu. The node carries a CC BY 4.0 licence, and its README restricts use to academic research and to the terms of the source image sets. We report statistics only and redistribute no image.");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "The images were read from the archive as data. We dropped 12 images that looked greyscale (mean absolute difference between the red and blue channels under 2), which left 9,988.");
    labelled(&mut d, "section", "sec:methods", "Methods");
    blk(&mut d, "subsection", "Colour properties");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "Each image was resized so its longest side is 256 pixels and converted to the OKLab colour space. Three properties were fixed in advance. Lightness is the mean OKLab L over the frame. Chroma is the mean OKLab chroma. The third is the natural logarithm of the correlated colour temperature of the image's mean colour, by McCamy's approximation, clipped to the range 1,500 to 25,000 kelvin. A higher value means a cooler, bluer image.");
    blk(&mut d, "subsection", "The gain");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "Content is the subcategory, coded as 134 indicator variables. The baseline model predicts memorability from subcategory alone. The full model adds the three colour properties, standardised, as linear terms. Both were scored by held-out R2 over 10 folds stratified by subcategory. The primary statistic is the gain");
    labelled(&mut d, "eq", "eq:gain", "G = R^2_{full} - R^2_{content}");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "with both terms computed on held-out images.");
    blk(&mut d, "subsection", "Within-subcategory correlation");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "For each property we removed the subcategory mean from the property and from memorability and took the Pearson correlation of the two residuals,");
    labelled(&mut d, "eq", "eq:rw", "r_w = corr(m - m_s, c - c_s)");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "where {m:m} is memorability, {m:c} the property and the subscript {m:s} marks the subcategory mean.");
    blk(&mut d, "subsection", "Nulls, intervals and correction");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "The control for the gain permutes the colour rows among images of the same subcategory, 1,000 times, and refits on the same folds. The control for each correlation permutes the property within subcategory, 1,000 times, and compares absolute values. Each p value is a p value from draws,");
    labelled(&mut d, "eq", "eq:p", "p = \\frac{k + 1}{n + 1}");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "with {m:k} the number of draws at or above the real value and {m:n} the number of draws. With 1,000 draws the smallest possible value is 0.001. Each 95% interval comes from 2,000 bootstrap resamples over images. The p values were corrected with the Benjamini-Hochberg correction {cite:bh1995} over the study's eight primary tests.");
    labelled(&mut d, "section", "sec:controls", "Controls and planted checks");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "The sanity control shuffles memorability scores across all images and refits the full model, 1,000 times. Its 95th percentile is an R2 of -0.012, so a model with no real signal scores below zero on held-out images, and the real content-only R2 of 0.558 is far above it.");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "The content-matched shuffle is the control that matters for the claim. It keeps each image's subject and score and gives it the colour of another image of the same subcategory. Its gains have a mean of -0.0002 and a 95th percentile of +0.0001. Any gain the real colours show beyond that comes from colour varying with memorability inside subcategories.");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "This test had no planted check: no known colour effect was inserted to measure what size the method can see. The shuffle bars stand in for it. A within-subcategory correlation has to exceed about 0.019 to clear its shuffle, so effects smaller than that are not excluded.");
    labelled(&mut d, "section", "sec:results", "Results");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "Adding lightness, chroma and colour temperature lifts held-out R2 by 0.0022 (95% interval 0.0009 to 0.0034), from 0.558 to 0.560; the content-matched shuffle reaches 0.0001 at its 95th percentile. The p value is 0.001, the smallest the 1,000 draws allow, and 0.003 after correction. A secondary model with six colour properties (adding colourfulness, the spread of lightness and hue entropy) gains 0.0033 over content.");
    labelled(&mut d, "tablecap", "tab:r2", "Held-out R2 of each model on 9,988 images, 10 folds. The gain is against the subcategory model.");
    attributed(&mut d, "thead", "", "Model¦Held-out R2¦Gain", "cols=0.52,0.24,0.24");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Subcategory only (134 labels)¦0.558¦none");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Subcategory and three colour properties¦0.560¦0.0022");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Subcategory and six colour properties¦0.561¦0.0033");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Three colour properties only¦0.096¦not applicable");
    attributed(&mut d, "row", "", "Shuffled memorability, 95th percentile¦-0.012¦not applicable", "last=1");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "Within a subcategory each property correlates weakly with memorability: lightness +0.027, chroma +0.041 and log colour temperature -0.056, each above a shuffle bar near 0.019. Table {ref:tab:props} gives the intervals and Figure {ref:fig:within} sets each value beside the same correlation taken across all images with no subject control. The squared correlation for colour temperature is 0.003, so it accounts for about 0.3 percent of the differences in memorability inside a subcategory.");
    labelled(&mut d, "tablecap", "tab:props", "Within-subcategory correlation of each property with memorability. Shuffle bar: 95th percentile of the absolute correlation over 1,000 within-subcategory permutations. Corrected p by Benjamini-Hochberg over eight tests.");
    attributed(&mut d, "thead", "", "Property¦r within¦95% interval¦Shuffle bar¦Corrected p¦r across", "cols=0.22,0.14,0.22,0.14,0.14,0.14");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Lightness¦+0.027¦+0.008 to +0.046¦0.019¦0.005¦-0.021");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Chroma¦+0.041¦+0.023 to +0.061¦0.019¦0.003¦+0.121");
    attributed(&mut d, "row", "", "Log colour temperature¦-0.056¦-0.076 to -0.035¦0.020¦0.003¦-0.307", "last=1");
    attributed(&mut d, "figure", "fig:within", "Correlation of each colour property with memorability. Filled diamonds: within subcategory, with 95% intervals; the grey band is the shuffle bar. Hollow circles: across all images with no subject control. Drawn from the run's result file.", "src=fig-within.png;width=0.8;alt=Three rows, one per colour property, each with a filled diamond and interval near zero for the within-subcategory correlation and a hollow circle for the across-content correlation, which for colour temperature sits far to the left at -0.31");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "Colour alone, with no subject label, predicts memorability at R2 0.096, about 44 times the gain it adds once subcategory is known. Across content, cooler images are less memorable at a correlation of -0.307; inside a subcategory the same correlation is -0.056. Most of the across-content link is carried by which subjects tend to be warm or cool.");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "The direction of the colour temperature link differs by category: +0.050 in food and between -0.041 and -0.113 in the other four. Figure {ref:fig:category} shows all three properties by category. These per-category values are secondary, carry no verdict and have no intervals in the result file.");
    attributed(&mut d, "figure", "fig:category", "Within-subcategory correlation by broad category for the three properties. Secondary values with no intervals. Drawn from the run's result file.", "src=fig-category.png;width=0.8;alt=Grouped bars for five categories showing within-subcategory correlations between about -0.11 and +0.11, with colour temperature negative everywhere except food");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "A check made after the run and outside the verdicts took each property with the other two held fixed. Each kept its sign: lightness +0.032, chroma +0.032 and log colour temperature -0.054, each above a shuffle bar near 0.019 or 0.020. Within subcategory, colour temperature and lightness correlate at +0.113.");
    labelled(&mut d, "section", "sec:discussion", "Discussion");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "The result agrees in direction with the weak colour link reported for earlier image sets and puts a size on it for MemCat with the subject controlled. A lift of 0.0022 in R2 against a baseline of 0.558 is a detectable effect of no practical use for predicting which photograph will be remembered. For the decision the study was run to inform, the answer was no: a colour control aimed at memorability is not worth building on this evidence.");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "The contrast between the two columns of Table {ref:tab:props} is the more useful part. A reader who correlated colour temperature with memorability across the whole set would find -0.307 and might call warmth memorable. Fixing the subcategory cuts that to under a fifth of its size. The same care applies to any image property that differs between subjects.");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "The remaining link should not be read as colour acting on memory. A subcategory such as a kind of landscape still mixes scenes, and a snowy scene differs from a green one in both colour and content. The food category runs opposite to the others, which fits a reading in which the property stands for what is in the picture.");
    labelled(&mut d, "section", "sec:limits", "Limitations");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "One image set and one memory task. Memorability here is recognition in a repeat-detection game over minutes, and it may not carry to memory in daily life or to other image sets.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "Subcategory is coarse content. Colour inside a subcategory can still stand for scene content, so the within-subcategory link is an upper bound on what colour itself contributes.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "The models are linear in three whole-frame averages. Colour layout, contrast between regions and nonlinear effects were not tested.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "The test had no planted check, so the smallest effect the method could detect is known only from the shuffle bars.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "The per-category values and the six-property gain are secondary and have no intervals or corrected p values.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "The correction was made over eight tests, four of them in parts of the study not published here.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "Three of the study's four tests are left out: one rests on private images, one was untestable, and one rests on data that is not public.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "Scoring was by our own code and was not blind. The check with the other two properties held fixed was made after the results were seen.");
    labelled(&mut d, "section", "sec:verified", "Verified");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "Verified: on 14 November 2026 the image and subcategory counts, the 12 dropped images, the four R2 values and both gains, the gain's interval, p and corrected p, the three within-subcategory correlations with their intervals, p values and corrected p values, the three across-content correlations, the fifteen per-category values, the five shuffle summaries and the five verdicts with their rules were re-read from the run's result file. They match the findings ledger. The three partial correlations were re-read from the post hoc file. The preregistration's SHA-256 matches the hash in the result file and in the lock file. Both figures were drawn by a script that reads only the result file. The study was not re-run for this paper, and the image files were not reopened. The literature was found by two searches run on 14 November 2026 and read at the level of abstracts, publisher pages and search summaries; the full texts were not read. The Benjamini and Hochberg paper is cited for the method only and was not searched or read for this paper.");
    labelled(&mut d, "section", "sec:corrections", "Corrections");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "14 November 2026: narrowed to the memorability test. The ledger entry also reports a seasonal test on one person's saved images, an untestable mood test and a word-and-image test; these are left out because they rest on private or non-public material or produced no result.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "14 November 2026: first published version.");
    labelled(&mut d, "section", "sec:repro", "Reproduction");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "Data: MemCat, Goetschalckx and Wagemans 2019, Open Science Framework node kvghu, https://osf.io/kvghu (node licence CC BY 4.0; the README restricts use to academic research and the source sets' terms). Paper doi:10.7717/peerj.8169.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "Drop images whose mean absolute red minus blue difference is under 2. Resize to a longest side of 256 pixels, convert to OKLab, and compute mean lightness, mean chroma and the log of the correlated colour temperature of the mean colour.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "Fit memorability on subcategory indicators with and without the three standardised properties, 10 folds stratified by subcategory, and compute eq. {ref:eq:gain}. Permute colour rows within subcategory 1,000 times for the control and bootstrap images 2,000 times for the interval.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "Compute eq. {ref:eq:rw} for each property, with 1,000 within-subcategory permutations and eq. {ref:eq:p}.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "Preregistration SHA-256: 88c8ff352124d95b75627c069404c979e04764424eb18f6bbbfdab1ef9d8f8c3. Plan SHA-256: 6017a182e90f6ff680a46954b0fa5c3f8a4ae931024480b8e1a90c87556c88a3. Run 20261029T232751Z.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "Cost: the analysis stage of the whole study, all four tests, took 271 s on one desktop computer. The image measurement was a separate earlier step and its time is not in the result file.");
    labelled(&mut d, "section", "sec:appendix", "Appendix A. The preregistered verdicts");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "Each verdict is a set of rules written before the run. A verdict passes only when every rule holds. Values are read from the run's result file. The study's other six verdicts belong to the tests left out of this paper: four failed, one was untestable and one passed.");
    labelled(&mut d, "tablecap", "tab:verdicts", "The five memorability verdicts and the rules that decided each one.");
    attributed(&mut d, "thead", "", "Verdict¦Deciding rules¦Values¦Holds", "cols=0.20,0.44,0.22,0.14");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Sanity¦content R2 above the shuffled-score 95th percentile¦0.558 against -0.012¦yes");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Gain¦gain above 0; interval's lower end above 0; gain above the content-matched 95th percentile; corrected p below 0.05¦0.0022; 0.0009; 0.0001; 0.003¦yes");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Lightness¦absolute r above its shuffle bar; corrected p below 0.05¦0.027 against 0.019; 0.005¦yes");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Chroma¦absolute r above its shuffle bar; corrected p below 0.05¦0.041 against 0.019; 0.003¦yes");
    attributed(&mut d, "row", "", "Colour temperature¦absolute r above its shuffle bar; corrected p below 0.05¦0.056 against 0.020; 0.003¦yes", "last=1");
    labelled(&mut d, "section", "sec:glossary", "Glossary");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "Each term is defined where it is first used in the text. The same definitions, with the field each comes from and what each excludes, are collected here.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:memorability}. The share of viewers who correctly recognise a photograph as a repeat when it comes round again in a stream of images, corrected for false alarms; it is a property measured per image across many viewers. Field: vision science and memory research. Excludes how well one person remembers, memory over days or years, and how much viewers like the image.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:preregistration}. A dated written plan for a study, fixed and hash-locked before any result is seen: the question, the data, the statistic, the controls, the forecasts and the kill conditions. Field: research method (metascience). Excludes a plan revised after looking at the outcome, and a protocol written only for the record after the run.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:verdict}. The pass or fail of one preregistered rule set against the run's numbers, read off by code. Field: research method (our usage). Excludes an interpretation written after reading the numbers.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:lightness (OKLab L)}. How light or dark a colour appears on a scale built to match human judgement, here averaged over every pixel of the image. Field: colour science. Excludes physical light intensity, and contrast between parts of the image.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:chroma}. How far a colour is from grey at the same lightness, here the mean distance from the grey axis in OKLab over the image; our measure of saturation. Field: colour science. Excludes hue, which says which colour it is, and lightness.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:correlated colour temperature}. The temperature in kelvin of the glowing body whose colour is closest to a given colour; low values look warm and orange, high values look cool and blue. Here it is computed from the image's mean colour. Field: colour science and lighting. Excludes the temperature of anything in the scene, and the white balance setting of the camera.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:held-out R2}. The share of the variation in a quantity that a model predicts on data it was not fitted to; 1 is perfect, 0 is no better than predicting the average, and it can fall below 0. Field: statistics and machine learning. Excludes R2 computed on the same data used to fit the model, which rises whenever a variable is added.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:control}. A version of the data with the structure under test removed (for example shuffled or pooled), scored the same way as the real data, so the real score has something to beat. Field: statistics and experimental design. Excludes a comparison with a different dataset or with a published number.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:p value (from draws)}. The share of control draws scoring at or above the real score, with one added to the count and to the number of draws, so it is never zero. Field: statistics. Excludes a p value from a textbook formula that assumes a distribution.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:95% interval}. The range that holds the middle 95 percent of the bootstrap or control draws for a number; we report it beside the number. Field: statistics. Excludes a statement that the true value lies inside with 95 percent probability.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:Benjamini-Hochberg correction}. An adjustment of a set of p values that holds the expected share of false discoveries among the results called significant at a chosen level. Field: statistics. Excludes a guarantee that no result in the set is a false discovery.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:planted check}. Data built so that the right answer is known, run through the same analysis before the real data, to show that the analysis can see the effect when it is there. Field: research method; called a positive control in experimental science. Excludes any check made on the real data, which cannot show what the analysis misses.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:findings ledger}. The append-only, dated list of every result we have recorded, numbered F1, F2 and on; corrections are added as new dated lines and old entries are never rewritten. Field: our records. Excludes a curated list of successes.");
    blk(&mut d, "section", "References");
    labelled(&mut d, "bib", "memcat2019", "Goetschalckx, L. and Wagemans, J. (2019). MemCat: a new category-based image set quantified on memorability. PeerJ 7:e8169. doi:10.7717/peerj.8169. Data: Open Science Framework node kvghu.");
    labelled(&mut d, "bib", "isola2014", "Isola, P., Xiao, J., Parikh, D., Torralba, A. and Oliva, A. (2014). What makes a photograph memorable? IEEE Transactions on Pattern Analysis and Machine Intelligence 36(7), 1469-1482. doi:10.1109/TPAMI.2013.200");
    labelled(&mut d, "bib", "bh1995", "Benjamini, Y. and Hochberg, Y. (1995). Controlling the false discovery rate: a practical and powerful approach to multiple testing. Journal of the Royal Statistical Society, Series B 57, 289-300.");
    let ok = save(&d, "F62.doc.tsv");
    println!("{} blocks ({})", d.blocks, ok);
}
